<section id=3D"S1" class=3D"ltx_section">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id=3D"S1.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S1.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">As large language models (LLMs) are impro=
ving in capabilities and instruction following&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite=
 ltx_citemacro_cite">Ouyang et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/=
2505.13360v3#bib.bib28" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2022</a>)</cite>, they=
 are increasingly integrated into commercial applications through customize=
d instruction <span id=3D"S1.p1.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pro=
mpts</span> that can span thousands of words&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite l=
tx_citemacro_cite">(e.g., All-Hands-AI, <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2=
505.13360v3#bib.bib2" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2025</a>; Anthropic, <a =
href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib3" title=3D"" class=3D"l=
tx_ref">2025</a>)</cite>.
Conveying nuanced intentions to LLMs, however, is inherently difficult.
This issue is less significant for end users, as their prompts are typicall=
y one-off and considered successful as long as they yield one satisfactory =
response throughout their interactions.
For LLM application <span id=3D"S1.p1.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_itali=
c">developers</span>, the problem is much more serious, as their prompts ne=
ed to generalize to many different usage scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S1.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S1.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p">As an example (Figure&nbsp;<a href=3D"htt=
ps://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S1.F1" title=3D"Figure 1 =E2=80=A3 1 Intro=
duction =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managin=
g Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_t=
ext ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), consider a developer building an LLM-powere=
d trip advisor:
The detailed prompt may specify the task&nbsp;=E2=9E=80, tone&nbsp;=E2=9E=
=81, and certain behaviors such as avoiding transactions&nbsp;=E2=9E=82 and=
 clarifying ambiguity&nbsp;=E2=9E=83, yet it remains <span id=3D"S1.p2.1.1"=
 class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">underspecified</span> in many aspects:
For example, it does not specify whether LLMs should warn about weather, pr=
oactively ask follow-up questions, or remind users of visa (entry) requirem=
ents.
If the LLM ends up not satisfying these requirements, it can cause frustrat=
ions and failures, such as users booking activities during bad weather, rec=
eiving vague recommendations, or facing denied entry due to visa issues, ul=
timately undermining trust in the LLM-powered applications.
Indeed, failing to mention prerequisites of suggested activities has alread=
y caused chaotic experiences in an existing LLM trip advisor&nbsp;<cite cla=
ss=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Bernal and Hoover (<a href=3D"https://ar=
xiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib7" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2024</a>)<=
/cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S1.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S1.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p">We define <span id=3D"S1.p3.1.1" class=3D=
"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">underspecification</span> as <span id=3D"S1.p3.1=
.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the omission of essential requirements=
 in a prompt, such that multiple valid but inconsistent behaviors remain po=
ssible.</span>
While it is possible that developers simply do not care enough to specify t=
hese behaviors, it is equally =E2=80=93 if not more =E2=80=93 likely that c=
urrent engineering and evaluation practices make it difficult for developer=
s to identify such underspecification until they have already led to issues=
 in deployment (Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3=
#S2" title=3D"2 Underspecification is Amplified in Prompting LLMs =E2=80=A3=
 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecificat=
ion in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">=
2</span></a>).
Even if developers do not believe these behaviors must be explicitly define=
d, they may expect models to act consistently along these dimensions to sup=
port more stable user mental models of LLM applications,
which is, however, not the case for many unspecified requirements (Figure&n=
bsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S1.F1" title=3D"Figure 1=
 =E2=80=A3 1 Introduction =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Underst=
anding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><s=
pan class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
