<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Developers often underspecify prompts and miss=
 user-important requirements, leading to divergent behaviors.
We analyze the challenges underspecification presents and propose mechanism=
s to manage the problem.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id=3D"S1.p4" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S1.p4.1" class=3D"ltx_p">In this work, we characterize (Section&nb=
sp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S2" title=3D"2 Underspeci=
fication is Amplified in Prompting LLMs =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99=
t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=
=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and empiric=
ally analyze (Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S=
3" title=3D"3 How do LLM+Prompts Behave when Underspecified? =E2=80=A3 What=
 Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification i=
n LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</sp=
an></a>) the problem of prompt underspecification.
To systematically study the problem, we curated a set of diverse requiremen=
ts across 3 representative tasks, constructed a series of prompts that spec=
ify different subsets of the requirements, and evaluated each requirement=
=E2=80=99s satisfaction rate with human-validated LLM-as-a-judge&nbsp;<cite=
 class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Zheng et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https:=
//arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib47" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2023<=
/a>)</cite>.
In total, we collected 8.4k data points of LLM+Prompts=E2=80=99 aggregated =
behaviors on diverse requirements.
Our analysis demonstrated that, <em id=3D"S1.p4.1.1" class=3D"ltx_emph ltx_=
font_italic">while LLMs can indeed often (41.1%) fill in the underspecifica=
tion gap, their behaviors are rather inconsistent:</em>
One version of an LLM may excel in fulfilling an unspecified requirement, b=
ut the next version can unexpectedly degrade by more than 20%.
This will be a problem for continuously developing, deploying, and maintain=
ing LLM-powered applications reliably.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S1.p5" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S1.p5.1" class=3D"ltx_p">We then introduce <span id=3D"S1.p5.1.1" =
class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">requirements-aware prompt optimization</=
span> as a solution strategy to deliberately communicate important requirem=
ents to the model, while leaving those already implicitly fulfilled unspeci=
fied (Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S4" title=
=3D"4 Requirements-Aware Prompt Optimization =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=
=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" =
class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
We show that such strategies overcome issues with existing approaches:
The obvious strategy of simply <span id=3D"S1.p5.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx=
_font_italic">specifying all requirements</span> in the prompt does not wor=
k, due to LLMs=E2=80=99 limited instruction-following capabilities =E2=80=
=93 their performance can drop by 19% as we specify more requirements (Sect=
ion&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S3.SS4" title=3D"3.=
4 LLMs struggle with following many requirements at the same time =E2=80=A3=
 3 How do LLM+Prompts Behave when Underspecified? =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Do=
n=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Promp=
ts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>),
and requirement-agnostic prompt optimization only provides limited help sin=
ce they have no requirement-specific feedback.
We propose and evaluate two <span id=3D"S1.p5.1.3" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_fo=
nt_italic">requirement-aware</span> prompt optimizers:
One to optimize <span id=3D"S1.p5.1.4" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">h=
ow</span> to specify requirements,
and the other to explicitly optimize <span id=3D"S1.p5.1.5" class=3D"ltx_te=
xt ltx_font_italic">what</span> requirements to specify.
We demonstrate that both strategies work well (+4.8% accuracy), and the lat=
ter can produce shorter prompts (-43% tokens) that are easier to follow for=
 the model.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S1.p6" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S1.p6.1" class=3D"ltx_p">Finally, we discuss how to <span id=3D"S1=
.p6.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">manage</span> prompt underspeci=
fication when building LLM applications in practice beyond prompt optimizat=
ion (Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S5" title=
=3D"5 Towards Managing Prompt Underspecification =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=
=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompt=
s" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>):
This includes proactively discovering important requirements, building reli=
able requirement evaluators, as well as continuously evaluating and monitor=
ing (un-)specified requirements.
We highlight the research opportunities here to support the entire process =
of managing prompt (under-)specification.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S1.p7" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S1.p7.1" class=3D"ltx_p">In summary, our work makes the following =
contribution:
<span id=3D"S1.p7.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> Characte=
rization of the prompt underspecification problem (Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"=
https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S2" title=3D"2 Underspecification is Am=
plified in Prompting LLMs =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Underst=
anding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><s=
pan class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>),
<span id=3D"S1.p7.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> an empir=
ical analysis of LLM+Prompt behaviors when underspecified (Section&nbsp;<a =
href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S3" title=3D"3 How do LLM+Promp=
ts Behave when Underspecified? =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Un=
derstanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_re=
f"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>),
<span id=3D"S1.p7.1.3" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3)</span> mitigati=
on mechanisms with <span id=3D"S1.p7.1.4" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic=
">requirements-aware</span> prompt optimizers (Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"http=
s://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S4" title=3D"4 Requirements-Aware Prompt Op=
timization =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Mana=
ging Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"lt=
x_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), and
<span id=3D"S1.p7.1.5" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(4)</span> a discus=
sion of our vision for managing prompt underspecification (Section&nbsp;<a =
href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S5" title=3D"5 Towards Managing=
 Prompt Underspecification =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Unders=
tanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><=
span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"S2" class=3D"ltx_section">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Underspecification is Ampl=
ified in Prompting LLMs</h2>

<div id=3D"S2.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S2.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">While underspecification is a known chall=
enge in traditional software engineering and machine learning pipelines&nbs=
p;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">K=C3=A4stner et&nbsp;al. (<a =
href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib17" title=3D"" class=3D"=
ltx_ref">2021</a>); D=E2=80=99Amour et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.o=
rg/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib11" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2022</a>)</cit=
e>,
it is significantly amplified in prompting LLMs.
We next elaborate on two key factors behind this amplification: (a) the lac=
k of rigor in prompt engineering practices, and (b) the rapid co-evolution =
of prompts and LLMs.
A summary of these differences is provided in Table&nbsp;<a href=3D"https:/=
/arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S2.T1" title=3D"Table 1 =E2=80=A3 2 Underspeci=
fication is Amplified in Prompting LLMs =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99=
t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=
=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
