iv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib11" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2022</a>)<=
/cite></span>
</span>
</td>
<td id=3D"S2.T1.1.5.4.4" class=3D"ltx_td ltx_align_justify ltx_align_top lt=
x_border_bb">
<span id=3D"S2.T1.1.5.4.4.1" class=3D"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id=3D"S2.T1.1.5.4.4.1.1" class=3D"ltx_p" style=3D"width:128.0pt;">Sof=
tware that does not meet customer needs; incorrect behavior in edge cases <=
cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_align_left ltx_centering ltx_citemacro_cite">Som=
merville (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib34" title=
=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2015</a>)</cite></span>
</span>
</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Tab=
le 1: </span>Compared to traditional ML models and software, LLM prompts ar=
e more prone to underspecification, less stable, and evolve more frequently=
.
Prompt engineering practices and further amplify these issues.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id=3D"S2.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S2.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S2.p2.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_font_bold">Ad-hoc prompt engineering amplifies underspecification.</spa=
n>
Prompts are developed with the expectation that not everything needs to be =
specified =E2=80=93 ideally, LLMs should behave like a human and fill in th=
e gaps with commonsense.
This expectation encourages a <span id=3D"S2.p2.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_=
font_italic">=E2=80=9Cminimal-specification=E2=80=9D</span> prompt engineer=
ing practice:
Developers begin with an initial prompt, observe violations of expected beh=
avior, and iteratively revise through adding more instructions (i.e., speci=
fications)&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Zamfirescu-Pere=
ira et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib42" =
title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2023</a>); Liang et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https=
://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib22" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2024=
</a>)</cite>.
This trial-and-error process lacks the rigor of traditional requirements en=
gineering&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Van&nbsp;Lamswee=
rde (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib38" title=3D"" c=
lass=3D"ltx_ref">2009</a>)</cite>, and exposes only a narrow slice of possi=
ble behaviors.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S2.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S2.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p">Yet many requirements demand more systema=
tic discovery efforts:
Some are <span id=3D"S2.p3.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">conditio=
nal</span> (e.g., a <span id=3D"S2.p3.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_itali=
c">=E2=80=9Cmentioning visa=E2=80=9D</span> requirement applies only to int=
ernational travel).
Others are <span id=3D"S2.p3.1.3" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">infreq=
uently violated yet critical</span> (e.g., failing <span id=3D"S2.p3.1.4" c=
lass=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">=E2=80=9Cno dangerous activities suggeste=
d=E2=80=9D</span> is high-stake even uncommon).
Still others are <span id=3D"S2.p3.1.5" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">=
difficult to recognize</span> (e.g., verifying <span id=3D"S2.p3.1.6" class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">=E2=80=9Csuggested sites are geographically c=
lose=E2=80=9D</span>).
Because such requirements are easily overlooked in ad-hoc iteration, unders=
pecification persists even as prompts appear to perform well on inspected e=
xamples.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S2.p4" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S2.p4.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S2.p4.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_font_bold">Rapid Prompt-LLM co-evolution amplifies underspecification.<=
/span>
Prompts are not static artifacts =E2=80=93 they are routinely modified to a=
dd new features, address failures, or adapt to updated LLMs&nbsp;<cite clas=
s=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Tafreshipour et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"http=
s://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib37" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">202=
4</a>)</cite>.
Because their specifications are written in natural language, such revision=
s are easy and quick to make, encouraging frequent, informal changes.
At the same time, LLMs themselves evolve rapidly, often without clear chang=
e logs, and sometimes silently without developers=E2=80=99 control&nbsp;<ci=
te class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Ma et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https:/=
/arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib24" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2024</=
a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S2.p5" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S2.p5.1" class=3D"ltx_p">This dual, fast-moving evolution of both =
prompts and models introduces continual behavioral drift:
Previously validated behaviors may no longer hold, requiring repeated redis=
covery of requirements.
The result is a growing maintenance burden for developers, who must continu=
ally re-evaluate prompt behavior to keep up with both axes of change.
In contrast, traditional software change is often carefully managed&nbsp;<c=
ite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">McConnell (<a href=3D"https://arx=
iv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib25" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">1998</a>);=
 Bogart et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib=
8" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2021</a>)</cite>, and most ML pipelines evo=
lve more slowly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"S3" class=3D"ltx_section">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>How do LLM+Prompts Behave =
when Underspecified?</h2>

<div id=3D"S3.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">The difficulty of reliably discovering un=
specified requirements raises a natural follow-up question:
What happens when such requirements are left out of the prompt?
While LLM+Prompts are generally known to be unstable, existing work mostly =
studies their stability on <span id=3D"S3.p1.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_fon=
t_italic">specified</span> requirements.
To quantitatively measure LLM+Prompts=E2=80=99 behaviors on <span id=3D"S3.=
p1.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unspecified</span> requirements,
we design an experiment as follows:
We collect a set of 60 plausible requirements across 3 tasks and create hum=
an-validated, automated validators for each requirement.
We then create prompts with subsets of the requirements and measure how wel=
l the model=E2=80=99s outputs meet the (un-)specified requirements with val=
idators.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p">Our analysis starts with showing that LLM=
s can often guess unspecified requirements, but these behaviors are inconsi=
stent (Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S3.SS2" =
title=3D"3.2 LLMs are often able to guess unspecified requirements but lack=
 stability =E2=80=A3 3 How do LLM+Prompts Behave when Underspecified? =E2=
=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspec=
ification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref=
_tag">3.2</span></a>) and more likely to degrade with model updates (Sectio=
n&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S3.SS3" title=3D"3.3 =
LLMs are more likely to regress on unspecified requirements when updated =
=E2=80=A3 3 How do LLM+Prompts Behave when Underspecified? =E2=80=A3 What P=
rompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in =
LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</sp=
an></a>).
We show that an obvious solution of specifying all requirements in a single=
 prompt actually hurts performance, due to LLMs=E2=80=99 limited ability to=
 follow long, complex instructions (Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.o=
rg/html/2505.13360v3#S3.SS4" title=3D"3.4 LLMs struggle with following many=
 requirements at the same time =E2=80=A3 3 How do LLM+Prompts Behave when U=
nderspecified? =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and =
Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id=3D"S3.SS1" class=3D"ltx_subsection">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experiment Setups</h3=
>

<div id=3D"S3.SS1.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS1.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Tasks and data.</span>
We selected three representative tasks based on Anthropic=E2=80=99s report =
measuring AI usage patterns&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite=
">Handa et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib=
14" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2025</a>)</cite>, covering different occup=
ational categories (Office, Business, and Computer).
These tasks reflect commercially integrable applications across multiple do=
mains, including software engineering (<span id=3D"S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class=3D"=
ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">code-explain</span>), the travel industry (<s=
pan id=3D"S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">trip-adviso=
ry</span>), and e-commerce (<span id=3D"S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class=3D"ltx_text lt=
x_font_typewriter">product-gen</span>).<span id=3D"footnote2" class=3D"ltx_=
note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class=3D"ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class=3D"=
ltx_note_outer"><span class=3D"ltx_note_content"><sup class=3D"ltx_note_mar=
k">2</sup><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>We analyzed two more=
 tasks, <span id=3D"footnote2.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">hea=
lth-consulting</span> and <span id=3D"footnote2.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_fo=
nt_typewriter">lesson-planning</span>, from different report categories (Ph=
ysical Sciences, Education), and shared the results in Appendix&nbsp;<a hre=
f=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A4" title=3D"Appendix D Additional=
 tasks setups and results =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Underst=
anding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><s=
pan class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS1.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS1.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p">We re-purposed three existing dataset=
s to run the LLM+Prompts on: Commitpackft&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_=
citemacro_cite">Muennighoff et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/=
2505.13360v3#bib.bib26" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2023</a>)</cite> for c=
ode explanation, a subset of UltraChat&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_cit=
emacro_cite">Ding et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360=
v3#bib.bib12" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2023</a>)</cite> for trip adviso=
ry, and Amazon ESCI&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Reddy =
et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib30" titl=
e=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2022</a>)</cite> for product description generatio=
n.
From each dataset, we sampled 200 examples, and split them into training, v=
alidation, and test data with 15/35/50 split. More details can be found at =
Appendix&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A1.SS1" title=
=3D"A.1 Task descriptions =E2=80=A3 Appendix A Details on Experiments Setup=
s =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Unde=
rspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text lt=
x_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>=E2=80=93&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2=
505.13360v3#A1.SS2" title=3D"A.2 Process of data sampling and cleaning =E2=
=80=A3 Appendix A Details on Experiments Setups =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=
=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompt=
s" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</=
p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS1.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS1.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Requirements.</span>
The key setup of our experiment is to curate a list of plausible requiremen=
ts for each task we study.
Note that there is no fixed set of requirements for a task, as different de=
velopers and application contexts may have different priorities.
We aim to curate requirements that are plausible and likely shared.
To this end, we explicitly cover various requirements elicitation methods&n=
bsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Van&nbsp;Lamsweerde (<a hre=
f=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib38" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx=
_ref">2009</a>)</cite> for our curation:</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS1.p4" class=3D"ltx_para">
<ul id=3D"S3.I1" class=3D"ltx_itemize">
<li id=3D"S3.I1.i1" class=3D"ltx_item" style=3D"list-style-type:none;">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">=E2=80=A2</span>=20
<div id=3D"S3.I1.i1.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Existing prompts.</span> For each task, we coll=
ected prompts from the Internet, covering ones provided by Anthropic, Googl=
e, and popular GPTs (prompts shared in Appendix&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arx=
iv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A1.SS4" title=3D"A.4 Prompts for requirements elic=
itation and validation =E2=80=A3 Appendix A Details on Experiments Setups =
=E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Unders=
pecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_=
ref_tag">A.4</span></a>). We instructed an LLM (<span id=3D"S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2=
" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o</span>) to extract <span id=
=3D"S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">specified</span> re=
quirements from each task prompt. This approach provides requirements that =
have been incorporated in real-world usage.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li id=3D"S3.I1.i2" class=3D"ltx_item" style=3D"list-style-type:none;">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">=E2=80=A2</span>=20
<div id=3D"S3.I1.i2.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Brainstorming.</span> For each task, we instruc=
ted an LLM (<span id=3D"S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewri=
ter">gpt-4o</span>) to analyze potential failure modes and propose requirem=
ents.
This simulates expert-driven elicitation methods by anticipating system fai=
lures&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Barzamini et&nbsp;al=
. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib5" title=3D"" clas=
s=3D"ltx_ref">2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li id=3D"S3.I1.i3" class=3D"ltx_item" style=3D"list-style-type:none;">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">=E2=80=A2</span>=20
<div id=3D"S3.I1.i3.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Error analysis.</span> For each task, we ran th=
e curated prompts on the train split with three smaller LLMs. We then instr=
ucted an LLM (<span id=3D"S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typew=
riter">gpt-4o</span>) to analyze their outputs and suggested missing requir=
ements.
This simulates error analysis to produce requirements grounded in mistakes.=
</p>
</div>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS1.p5" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS1.p5.1" class=3D"ltx_p">For the elicited requirements, we dro=
pped near duplicates, filtered out the ones that were overly specific, and =
finally had three independent annotators select the ones they found importa=
nt for the task.
We kept the requirements selected by at least one human annotator, with 20 =
requirements per task.
The final list of requirements covers different categories (content, style,=
 format) and scopes, as visualized in Figure&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.=
org/html/2505.13360v3#A1.F5" title=3D"Figure 5 =E2=80=A3 A.3 Process of req=
uirements curation =E2=80=A3 Appendix A Details on Experiments Setups =E2=
=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspec=
ification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref=
_tag">5</span></a>.
The full process and curated requirements are shared in Appendix&nbsp;<a hr=
ef=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A1.SS3" title=3D"A.3 Process of r=
equirements curation =E2=80=A3 Appendix A Details on Experiments Setups =E2=
=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspec=
ification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref=
_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS1.p6" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS1.p6.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Requirement validators.</span>
For each model output, we validate how well they satisfy each (specified or=
 unspecified) requirement, either using a Python script or an LLM validator=
&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Zheng et&nbsp;al. (<a hre=
f=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib47" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx=
_ref">2023</a>)</cite>.
We manually validate the LLM validators with a sampled test subset, achievi=
ng an average of 95.6% human-LLM agreement.
More details can be found in Appendix&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/htm=
l/2505.13360v3#A1.SS6" title=3D"A.6 Human validation of requirement validat=
ors =E2=80=A3 Appendix A Details on Experiments Setups =E2=80=A3 What Promp=
ts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM =
Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span><=
/a>.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS1.p7" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS1.p7.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompts.</span>
From the curated requirements, we generate prompts that each include a subs=
et of these requirements.
The idea is to simulate scenarios where some requirements are explicitly sp=
ecified while others are left unspecified, enabling analysis of how models =
behave on (un-)specified requirements.
We use a cyclic design to construct prompts, where each one includes N cons=
ecutive requirements (with N set to 10; see Figure&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://=
arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A1.F11" title=3D"Figure 11 =E2=80=A3 A.7 Prompt=
 templates and construction =E2=80=A3 Appendix A Details on Experiments Set=
ups =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Un=
derspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).
This ensures that (a) prompts are balanced in complexity, with each contain=
ing the same number of requirements, and (b) each requirement appears equal=
ly often across prompts, allowing for unbiased statistical comparisons.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS1.p8" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS1.p8.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric.</span>
For each requirement, we measure its satisfaction rate on each LLM+Prompt c=
ombination when specified or unspecified.
We aggregate the results across prompts or models for analysis.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"S3.SS2" class=3D"ltx_subsection">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>LLMs are often able t=
o guess unspecified requirements but lack stability</h3>

<div id=3D"S3.SS2.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS2.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Setups.</span>
We study three models, one smaller open-sourced model <span id=3D"S3.SS2.p1=
.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct</span>,
one big closed-sourced model <span id=3D"S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class=3D"ltx_text l=
tx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o-2024-08-06</span>,
and one reasoning model <span id=3D"S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_fo=
nt_typewriter">o3-mini</span>.
We calculate the average accuracy and standard deviation for each requireme=
nt when specified or unspecified, and compare their distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS2.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS2.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
Unsurprisingly, we generally observe that LLM+Prompts are less likely to im=
plement a requirement when unspecified =E2=80=93 accuracy drops by an avera=
ge of 22.6% (and up to 93.1%) compared to when the requirement is explicitl=
y stated (Figure <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S3.F2" titl=
e=3D"Figure 2 =E2=80=A3 3.2 LLMs are often able to guess unspecified requir=
ements but lack stability =E2=80=A3 3 How do LLM+Prompts Behave when Unders=
pecified? =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Manag=
ing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx=
_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
Yet, not all requirements are equally affected.
We found that <em id=3D"S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class=3D"ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">L=
LMs are often able to guess unspecified requirements</em> =E2=80=93 in 41.1=
% of cases, they are able to achieve more than 98% accuracy on unspecified =
requirements.
Indeed, for 65% of all requirements, we found at least one LLM+prompt combi=
nation that is able to guess it without explicit specification.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS2.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS2.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p">Comparing across models, stronger LLM=
s are more likely to guess requirements:
<span id=3D"S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">o3-mini</=
span> can guess 44.7% of unspecified requirements, a 20.2% increase compare=
d to <span id=3D"S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llam=
a3.3-70b-instruct</span>,
possibly due to extra test-time compute to =E2=80=9Cinfer=E2=80=9D requirem=
ents in reasoning.</p>
</div>
<figure id=3D"S3.F2" class=3D"ltx_figure"><img src=3D"https://arxiv.org/htm=
l/2505.13360v3/x2.png" id=3D"S3.F2.g1" class=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centering =
ltx_img_landscape" width=3D"747" height=3D"391" alt=3D"Refer to caption">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>While LLMs perform worse (-22.6% avg.) when a =
requirement is unspecified (top), they are often (41.1% avg.) able to guess=
 unspecified requirements (<math id=3D"S3.F2.2.m1" class=3D"ltx_Math" altte=
xt=3D"\geq 0.98" display=3D"inline" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><m=
i></mi><mo>=E2=89=A5</mo><mn>0.98</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"applic=
ation/x-tex">\geq 0.98</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy), with incr=
eased capabilities (bottom). </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id=3D"S3.SS2.p4" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS2.p4.1" class=3D"ltx_p">Breaking down the results, we found <=
em id=3D"S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class=3D"ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">LLMs are especia=
lly good at guessing format-related requirements</em> (70.7% vs. 41.1% on a=
verage).
For example, models are often able to <span id=3D"S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_italic">=E2=80=9Cprovide a high-level summary at the begin=
ning=E2=80=9D</span> or <span id=3D"S3.SS2.p4.1.3" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_fo=
nt_italic">=E2=80=9Cavoid special characters=E2=80=9D</span> by default.
This is likely because these requirements are more universal and therefore =
have been built into LLMs natively in post-training.
Meanwhile, LLMs struggle much more with <span id=3D"S3.SS2.p4.1.4" class=3D=
"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">conditional</span> requirements (22.9% vs. 41.1%=
 ),
as these requirements often specify corner cases that are hard to predict (=
e.g., <span id=3D"S3.SS2.p4.1.5" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">=E2=80=
=9Cprovide warnings about weather conditions=E2=80=9D</span>).
Somewhat surprisingly, 65.2% requirements found from existing developer pro=
mpts are guessed by LLMs when unspecified.
This indicates that the prompts resulting from existing practices often con=
tain information that might be redundant to LLMs=E2=80=99 default behaviors=
.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS2.p5" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS2.p5.1" class=3D"ltx_p">While LLMs are often able to guess un=
specified requirements, we found them <em id=3D"S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class=3D"ltx=
_emph ltx_font_italic">less robust with unspecified requirements across dif=
ferent prompts</em>:
Different prompts can guess unspecified requirements completely differently=
.
On average, they have a standard deviation of 8.9%, a more than 2x increase=
 compared to when they are specified.
We observe even a stronger effect when explicitly controlling for requireme=
nt conflicts (7.9% unspecified vs. 0.8% specification, details in Appendix&=
nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A3.SS3" title=3D"C.3 Re=
quirements Conflict Analysis =E2=80=A3 Appendix C Additional experiment res=
ults =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing U=
nderspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text=
 ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS2.p6" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS2.p6.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Implications.</span>
While LLMs do follow many unspecified requirements, their success depends o=
n how a specific model is post-trained.
Developers seem to struggle with identifying ones that need to be specified=
.
This justifies a need for automated exploration of what to specify (Section=
&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S4.SS2" title=3D"4.2 D=
esigning requirement-aware prompt optimizers =E2=80=A3 4 Requirements-Aware=
 Prompt Optimization =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understandin=
g and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span c=
lass=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) and properly managing and tes=
ting all relevant requirements, whether specified in the prompt or not (Sec=
tion&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S5" title=3D"5 Tow=
ards Managing Prompt Underspecification =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99=
t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=
=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"S3.SS3" class=3D"ltx_subsection">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>LLMs are more likely =
to regress on unspecified requirements when updated</h3>

<figure id=3D"S3.F3" class=3D"ltx_figure"><img src=3D"https://arxiv.org/htm=
l/2505.13360v3/x3.png" id=3D"S3.F3.g1" class=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centering =
ltx_img_landscape" width=3D"664" height=3D"335" alt=3D"Refer to caption">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Cumulative distribution of accuracy drop (trun=
cated at 0%). Prompts regress more on unspecified requirements across model=
 updates, with an almost 2x increase compared to specified requirements.</f=
igcaption>
</figure>
<div id=3D"S3.SS3.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS3.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Setups.</span>
To analyze model updates, we study six models from <span id=3D"S3.SS3.p1.1.=
2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o</span> and <span id=3D"S3.=
SS3.p1.1.3" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">llama-3</span> model fam=
ilies:
Three versions of <span id=3D"S3.SS3.p1.1.4" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typ=
ewriter">gpt-4o</span>: <span id=3D"S3.SS3.p1.1.5" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_fo=
nt_typewriter">05-13</span>, <span id=3D"S3.SS3.p1.1.6" class=3D"ltx_text l=
tx_font_typewriter">08-06</span>, and <span id=3D"S3.SS3.p1.1.7" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_typewriter">11-20</span> for simulating a hidden drift of =
model versions, and three versions of <span id=3D"S3.SS3.p1.1.8" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3-70B-Instruct</span>: <span id=3D"S3.SS=
3.p1.1.9" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3</span>, <span id=
=3D"S3.SS3.p1.1.10" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.1</span>=
, and <span id=3D"S3.SS3.p1.1.11" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ll=
ama-3.3</span>, simulating intentional model migration and bigger changes.
For each potential update within the same model family, we calculate the ac=
curacy change for each prompt and requirement.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS3.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS3.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
We found that while the majority of model updates result in stable behavior=
s (&lt;1% changes) or improvement, there are still a significant portion (2=
2.9%) of cases where LLMs regress.
Breaking down the regressions, we found that prompts regress more often on =
<span id=3D"S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unspecified</=
span> requirements: 5.9% requirements regress more than 20% over model upda=
tes when they are unspecified =E2=80=93 an almost 2x increase compared to s=
pecified requirements (Figure&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.1=
3360v3#S3.F3" title=3D"Figure 3 =E2=80=A3 3.3 LLMs are more likely to regre=
ss on unspecified requirements when updated =E2=80=A3 3 How do LLM+Prompts =
Behave when Underspecified? =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Under=
standing and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref">=
<span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
This is true even for small hidden model updates,
=E2=80=93 e.g., updating <span id=3D"S3.SS3.p2.1.3" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_f=
ont_typewriter">gpt-4o</span> from <span id=3D"S3.SS3.p2.1.4" class=3D"ltx_=
text ltx_font_typewriter">05-13</span> to <span id=3D"S3.SS3.p2.1.5" class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">08-06</span> makes it 48% less likely to =
<span id=3D"S3.SS3.p2.1.6" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">=E2=80=9Cprod=
uce skimmable outputs,=E2=80=9D</span> and 14% less likely to <span id=3D"S=
3.SS3.p2.1.7" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">=E2=80=9Cmention customer =
support information=E2=80=9D</span> on average.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS3.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS3.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Implications.</span>
LLM updates are more likely to improve on specified requirements (through s=
tronger instruction-following) but can hurt unspecified requirements (with =
different default behaviors).
Regressions of unspecified requirements are both more frequent and far hard=
er to detect.
This makes it necessary to regularly evaluate and monitor known unspecified=
 requirements (Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#=
S5" title=3D"5 Towards Managing Prompt Underspecification =E2=80=A3 What Pr=
ompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in L=
LM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span>=
</a>).
Existing practices of manual inspection (=E2=80=9Cvibe check=E2=80=9D) of a=
 few examples will not be sustainable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"S3.SS4" class=3D"ltx_subsection">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>LLMs struggle with fo=
llowing many requirements at the same time</h3>

<div id=3D"S3.SS4.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS4.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">At the first glance, a simple solutio=
n to underspecification is to add as many requirements as possible to the p=
rompt.
We show that this is actually an anti-pattern that leads to over-complicate=
d prompts, and does not scale to many requirements due to LLMs=E2=80=99 lim=
ited instruction-following capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS4.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS4.p2.3" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS4.p2.3.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Setups.</span>
We generated additional prompts containing different numbers of requirement=
s (<math id=3D"S3.SS4.p2.1.m1" class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"N" display=3D"=
inline" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=3D"ap=
plication/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>=3D1, 5, 10, and 19), fol=
lowing the same method described in Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.o=
rg/html/2505.13360v3#S3.SS1" title=3D"3.1 Experiment Setups =E2=80=A3 3 How=
 do LLM+Prompts Behave when Underspecified? =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=
=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" =
class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
We study their behaviors on two models, <span id=3D"S3.SS4.p2.3.2" class=3D=
"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct</span> and <span id=
=3D"S3.SS4.p2.3.3" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o-2024-08-06=
</span>, and calculate the average accuracy on <math id=3D"S3.SS4.p2.2.m2" =
class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"N" display=3D"inline" intent=3D":literal"><se=
mantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">N</annotation>=
</semantics></math> specified and <math id=3D"S3.SS4.p2.3.m3" class=3D"ltx_=
Math" alttext=3D"20-N" display=3D"inline" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><m=
row><mn>20</mn><mo>=E2=88=92</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"a=
pplication/x-tex">20-N</annotation></semantics></math> unspecified requirem=
ents for each prompt.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS4.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS4.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
First, we found that LLMs are mostly able to follow specified requirements =
individually,
with an average of 98.7% accuracy.
This can be thought of as an approximate upper bound on performance, assumi=
ng each requirement could be stated in isolation without interference.
Next, we found LLMs=E2=80=99 average accuracy starts to drop with more requ=
irements specified (Figure&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.1336=
0v3#S3.F4" title=3D"Figure 4 =E2=80=A3 3.4 LLMs struggle with following man=
y requirements at the same time =E2=80=A3 3 How do LLM+Prompts Behave when =
Underspecified? =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and=
 Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>):
Specifying 19 requirements together yields only an 85.0% average accuracy f=
or <span id=3D"S3.SS4.p3.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o=
</span>.
Smaller models like <span id=3D"S3.SS4.p3.1.3" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_t=
ypewriter">Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct</span> struggle even more, with only 79.7=
% average accuracy.</p>
</div>
<figure id=3D"S3.F4" class=3D"ltx_figure"><img src=3D"https://arxiv.org/htm=
l/2505.13360v3/x4.png" id=3D"S3.F4.g1" class=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centering =
ltx_img_landscape" width=3D"747" height=3D"407" alt=3D"Refer to caption">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>LLMs=E2=80=99 average accuracy on <span id=3D"=
S3.F4.3.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">specified</span> requirements=
 drops with more requirements specified in the prompt, especially for small=
er models like <span id=3D"S3.F4.4.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter=
">Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id=3D"S3.SS4.p4" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS4.p4.1" class=3D"ltx_p">Breaking down the results, we found 3=
7.5% of requirements drop significantly by more than 5% on average (Figure&=
nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A3.F14" title=3D"Figure=
 14 =E2=80=A3 Appendix C Additional experiment results =E2=80=A3 What Promp=
ts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM =
Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></=
a>).
A few of these requirements suffer from inherent conflicts =E2=80=93 e.g., =
making product descriptions more skimmable conflicts with other formatting =
requirements.
However, we also found many cases without obvious conflicts, from <span id=
=3D"S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">=E2=80=9Cmentioning a=
vailability of transportation options=E2=80=9D</span> (-63.9% on <span id=
=3D"S3.SS4.p4.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.3-70B-Ins=
truct</span>) to <span id=3D"S3.SS4.p4.1.3" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_ital=
ic">=E2=80=9Cuse analogies and examples=E2=80=9D</span> (-81.3% on <span id=
=3D"S3.SS4.p4.1.4" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o-2024-08-06=
</span>).
We attribute these cases to LLMs=E2=80=99 limited instruction-following cap=
abilities: With the number of requirements increasing, it is much easier to=
 neglect some requirements and harder to satisfy all at the same time.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S3.SS4.p5" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S3.SS4.p5.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S3.SS4.p5.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Implications.</span>
As LLMs struggle with prompts with too many requirements, intentional under=
specification can be a strategy to focus the model only on select requireme=
nts without distracting it with requirements it follows by default. However=
, developers currently have no support for intentionally underspecifying th=
eir prompts (and are bad at this, see Section <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/=
html/2505.13360v3#S3.SS2" title=3D"3.2 LLMs are often able to guess unspeci=
fied requirements but lack stability =E2=80=A3 3 How do LLM+Prompts Behave =
when Underspecified? =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understandin=
g and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span c=
lass=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), which calls for automaticall=
y optimizing prompt specification (Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.or=
g/html/2505.13360v3#S4" title=3D"4 Requirements-Aware Prompt Optimization =
=E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Unders=
pecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_=
ref_tag">4</span></a>), especially as the number of requirements grows over=
 time.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id=3D"S4" class=3D"ltx_section">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Requirements-Aware Prompt =
Optimization</h2>

<div id=3D"S4.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S4.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">How can we improve LLMs=E2=80=99 performa=
nce despite their limited capabilities to follow complex instructions?
Inspired by recent trend on automatically improving prompts&nbsp;<cite clas=
s=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Khattab et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://a=
rxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib18" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2023</a>=
)</cite>,
we test whether existing optimizers can already mitigate this issue, and fo=
und that they provide inconsistent improvements.
We then introduce two <span id=3D"S4.p1.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bol=
d">requirement-aware</span> prompt optimizers:
We first enhance an existing prompt optimizer with requirement-specific eva=
luators, showing that requirement-specific feedback is valuable.
Inspired by our analysis in Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/=
2505.13360v3#S3" title=3D"3 How do LLM+Prompts Behave when Underspecified? =
=E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Unders=
pecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_=
ref_tag">3</span></a>, we also explore whether we can optimize <span id=3D"=
S4.p1.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">what</span> requirements to s=
pecify in the prompts, removing ones that are distracting or followed by de=
fault.
We propose a simple Bayesian prompt optimizer to efficiently search for a g=
ood requirement combination.</p>
</div>
