<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_table">Table 2: </span>Prompt optimization results on 60 different prom=
pts. We found both requirement-aware optimizers (COPRO-R and Bayesian) can =
consistently improve prompt performance (+4.8% on average), with the Bayesi=
an optimizer reducing token usage by 41 - 45%.
The trend holds for real-world prompts in agentic coding context. </figcapt=
ion>
</figure>
<section id=3D"S4.SS1" class=3D"ltx_subsection">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Existing prompt optim=
izers do not improve performance consistently</h3>

<div id=3D"S4.SS1.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S4.SS1.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">We first explore whether we can impro=
ve prompts with existing prompt optimizers. We use two off-the-shelf prompt=
 optimizers here:
<span id=3D"S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> Open=
AI=E2=80=99s prompt optimizer&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_ci=
te">OpenAI (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib27" title=
=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2025</a>)</cite>: This is a =E2=80=9Cstatic=E2=80=
=9D prompt optimizer that takes in a prompt and tries to improve it without=
 any model execution feedback.
<span id=3D"S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> DSPy=
=E2=80=99s COPRO optimizer&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"=
>Khattab et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bi=
b18" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2023</a>)</cite>: This represents a =E2=
=80=9Cdynamic=E2=80=9D prompt optimizer that iteratively proposes and explo=
res new prompts and finds ones with higher performance.
To guide the optimization, we use an LLM evaluator that scores outputs from=
 1 to 10, based on how well they adhere to the input prompts.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S4.SS1.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S4.SS1.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Setups.</span>
We apply all optimizers to prompts with the most requirements (N=3D19) and =
an LLM with weaker instruction-following capabilities <span id=3D"S4.SS1.p2=
.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3.3-70b-instruct</span>.
We perform prompt optimization on the train split (n=3D30) of each task dat=
aset, and evaluate the results on the test split.
All dynamic prompt optimizers are given a budget of 9 prompts to explore.
We measure average requirement accuracy and prompt token usage.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S4.SS1.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S4.SS1.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
Overall, we do not observe consistent improvements from the optimizers (Tab=
le&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S4.T2" title=3D"Tabl=
e 2 =E2=80=A3 4 Requirements-Aware Prompt Optimization =E2=80=A3 What Promp=
ts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM =
Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a=
>, OpenAI and COPRO).
While they provide small improvements on two tasks (+2.8% on average), they=
 also drop prompt performance on the <span id=3D"S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class=3D"lt=
x_text ltx_font_typewriter">trip-advisory</span> task (-1.1% on average).</=
p>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"S4.SS2" class=3D"ltx_subsection">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Designing requirement=
-aware prompt optimizers</h3>

<div id=3D"S4.SS2.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S4.SS2.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">Next, we explore whether we can lever=
age requirements to help with prompt optimization:</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S4.SS2.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S4.SS2.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">COPRO-R.</span>
We first enhance COPRO with requirement-specific validators to guide its op=
timization, providing average accuracy of <span id=3D"S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span> requirements (whether they are spe=
cified or not in the prompt) as the optimization metric.
We expect this helps optimizers obtain more accurate feedback and generate =
prompts that consider different requirements thoroughly.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S4.SS2.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S4.SS2.p3.3" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S4.SS2.p3.3.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Bayesian.</span>
We then explore optimizing <span id=3D"S4.SS2.p3.3.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx=
_font_italic">what</span> requirements to specify in a prompt.
This is from our observations that many requirements are actually redundant=
, followed by default, and do not need to be specified (Section&nbsp;<a hre=
f=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S3.SS2" title=3D"3.2 LLMs are ofte=
n able to guess unspecified requirements but lack stability =E2=80=A3 3 How=
 do LLM+Prompts Behave when Underspecified? =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=
=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" =
class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).
To explore an exponential number of requirement combinations, we propose a =
simple Bayesian prompt optimizer:
We model each requirement as a binary hyperparameter (specified vs. unspeci=
fied), with a configuration <math id=3D"S4.SS2.p3.1.m1" class=3D"ltx_Math" =
alttext=3D"r=3D(r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{n})\in\{0,1\}^{n}" display=3D"inline"=
 intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=3D</mo><mrow><mo stret=
chy=3D"false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r<=
/mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=3D"normal">=E2=80=A6</mi><mo=
>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow=
><mo>=E2=88=88</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,=
</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=3D"false">}</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow>=
<annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">r=3D(r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{n})\i=
n\{0,1\}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> indicating what requirements t=
o specify.
Given a performance function <math id=3D"S4.SS2.p3.2.m2" class=3D"ltx_Math"=
 alttext=3D"f(r)" display=3D"inline" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><=
mi>f</mi><mo lspace=3D"0em" rspace=3D"0em">=E2=80=8B</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=
=3D"false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><anno=
tation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">f(r)</annotation></semantics></math>,=
 the optimizer aims to solve the objective:
<math id=3D"S4.SS2.p3.3.m3" class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"r^{*}=3D\arg\max_=
{r\in\{0,1\}^{n}}f(r)" display=3D"inline" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><m=
row><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>=E2=88=97</mo></msup><mo>=3D</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ar=
g</mi><mo lspace=3D"0.167em">=E2=81=A1</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><m=
i>r</mi><mo>=E2=88=88</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">{</mo><mn>0</m=
n><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=3D"false">}</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup>=
</mrow></msub><mo lspace=3D"0.167em">=E2=81=A1</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow>=
<mo lspace=3D"0em" rspace=3D"0em">=E2=80=8B</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false=
">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annota=
tion encoding=3D"application/x-tex">r^{*}=3D\arg\max_{r\in\{0,1\}^{n}}f(r)<=
/annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S4.SS2.p4" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S4.SS2.p4.2" class=3D"ltx_p">We define <math id=3D"S4.SS2.p4.1.m1"=
 class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"f(r)" display=3D"inline" intent=3D":literal"=
><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace=3D"0em" rspace=3D"0em">=E2=80=8B</mo=
><mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo>=
</mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">f(r)</annotation><=
/semantics></math> as average requirement accuracy for our experiment, and =
leverage a classic Bayesian optimization algorithm, Tree-structured Parzen =
Estimator&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Bergstra et&nbsp=
;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib6" title=3D"" c=
lass=3D"ltx_ref">2011</a>)</cite>, to efficiently find a good <math id=3D"S=
4.SS2.p4.2.m2" class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"r" display=3D"inline" intent=
=3D":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-te=
x">r</annotation></semantics></math> in a small number of trials.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S4.SS2.p5" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S4.SS2.p5.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
We found that both requirement-aware optimizers can consistently improve pr=
ompt performance (Table&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3=
#S4.T2" title=3D"Table 2 =E2=80=A3 4 Requirements-Aware Prompt Optimization=
 =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Under=
specification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx=
_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
The simple Bayesian optimizer improves prompt performance by 3.8%, while re=
ducing token usage by 41 - 45%.
Inspecting the produced prompts, we found that global, format, and develope=
r-written requirements are more likely to be dropped (56.5%, 77.0%, 57.9% r=
espectively vs. 52.8% avg.), which aligns well with our observations in Sec=
tion&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S3.SS2" title=3D"3=
.2 LLMs are often able to guess unspecified requirements but lack stability=
 =E2=80=A3 3 How do LLM+Prompts Behave when Underspecified? =E2=80=A3 What =
Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in=
 LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</s=
pan></a> that these requirements are more likely to be guessed by an LLM.</=
p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S4.SS2.p6" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S4.SS2.p6.1" class=3D"ltx_p">Pairing requirement-specific validato=
rs with existing optimizers, we found COPRO-R can also improve performance =
by 5.8%.
Inspecting the produced prompts (Figure&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/h=
tml/2505.13360v3#A3.F16" title=3D"Figure 16 =E2=80=A3 C.6 Prompt Optimizati=
on Examples =E2=80=A3 Appendix C Additional experiment results =E2=80=A3 Wh=
at Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification=
 in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16<=
/span></a>), we found they reorder requirements in a more logical structure=
, merge related requirements together, and sometimes drop requirements from=
 the list.
These together produce a better way to specify a longer list of requirement=
s.
In contrast, the Bayesian optimizer offers a complementary strength to impr=
ove performance by deciding what <span id=3D"S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class=3D"ltx_te=
xt ltx_font_italic">subset</span> of requirements to specify.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S4.SS2.p7" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S4.SS2.p7.1" class=3D"ltx_p">In addition, we found our optimizers =
generalize well to real-world contexts:
On the agentic coding task of <span id=3D"S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_font_typewriter">website-gen</span> (see Appendix&nbsp;<a href=3D"https=
://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A5" title=3D"Appendix E Details on Agentic C=
oding Case Study =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding an=
d Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> for setup details), we found both op=
timizers are able to significantly improve baseline prompt shared in the Cu=
rsor community, with the Bayesian optimizer reducing token usage by 71.9%.<=
/p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id=3D"S5" class=3D"ltx_section">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Towards Managing Prompt Un=
derspecification</h2>

<div id=3D"S5.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S5.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">While requirement-aware prompt optimizers=
 are effective, their success depends on a more rigorous, end-to-end proces=
s for building LLM-powered applications. Drawing on insights from software =
engineering&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Sommerville (<=
a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib34" title=3D"" class=
=3D"ltx_ref">2015</a>)</cite>, we outline a structured approach to managing=
 underspecification =E2=80=93 where prompt developers can <span id=3D"S5.p1=
.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">discover</span> key task requireme=
nts, <span id=3D"S5.p1.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">curate</span=
> them to reflect real needs, and continuously <span id=3D"S5.p1.1.3" class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">monitor</span> for drift.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S5.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S5.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S5.p2.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_font_bold">Elicit requirements for comprehensive task representation, b=
y increasing requirement discoverability.</span>
As emphasized in the software engineering literature&nbsp;<cite class=3D"lt=
x_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Van&nbsp;Lamsweerde (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.or=
g/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib38" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2009</a>)</cite=
>, requirement elicitation is foundational for application development.
However, as discussed in Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/250=
5.13360v3#S2" title=3D"2 Underspecification is Amplified in Prompting LLMs =
=E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Unders=
pecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_=
ref_tag">2</span></a>, identifying task-specific requirements remains nontr=
ivial.
While our experiments take early steps toward automating this process (Sect=
ion&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S3.SS1" title=3D"3.=
1 Experiment Setups =E2=80=A3 3 How do LLM+Prompts Behave when Underspecifi=
ed? =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Un=
derspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), the broader challenge is to <em id=3D"S5.p2.1=
.2" class=3D"ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">maximize</em> requirement discoverab=
ility.
Future work may explore using synthetic data generation&nbsp;<cite class=3D=
"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Zhao et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.or=
g/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib46" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2024</a>)</cite=
> to surface edge cases or probe specific requirements.
We may also explore more traditional requirement engineering approaches, in=
cluding top-down brainstorming&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_c=
ite">Yang et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.b=
ib40" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2023</a>)</cite>, bottom-up data-driven =
analysis&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Zeng et&nbsp;al. =
(<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib43" title=3D"" class=
=3D"ltx_ref">2025</a>)</cite>,
or structrued safety engineering approaches that anticipate problems before=
 they occur&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Hong et&nbsp;a=
l. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib15" title=3D"" cl=
ass=3D"ltx_ref">2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S5.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S5.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S5.p3.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_font_bold">Select requirements that matter, via continuous monitoring o=
n the full requirement set.</span>
LLM behaviors drift over time&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_ci=
te">Chen et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bi=
b10" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2024</a>)</cite> and they have different =
capabilities to follow requirements that are specified and guess ones that =
are not (Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S3.SS2=
" title=3D"3.2 LLMs are often able to guess unspecified requirements but la=
ck stability =E2=80=A3 3 How do LLM+Prompts Behave when Underspecified? =E2=
=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspec=
ification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref=
_tag">3.2</span></a>).
Deciding what requirements to specify and how to specify them (Section&nbsp=
;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S4" title=3D"4 Requirements=
-Aware Prompt Optimization =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Unders=
tanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><=
span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), therefore, is largely mo=
del-dependent.
To support model migrations, we recommend background validation of all trac=
ked requirements.
This allows detection of drift and allows developers or optimizers to re-se=
lect which requirements to explicitly specify when switching models, from a=
 stable overarching set.
This requires running validations for each new prompt or model and alerting=
 developers of significant changes.
We could further distribute requirements across sub-modules in workflows or=
 multi-agent systems&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Zhou =
et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib48" titl=
e=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2025</a>)</cite> to enabling more local monitoring=
 and optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S5.p4" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S5.p4.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S5.p4.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_font_bold">Curate requirements to be more aligned with developer needs,=
 by validating the validators.</span>
Both optimization and monitoring rely on robust requirement validators.
While validators can be separately tuned (e.g., we performed manual validat=
ion), a more rigorous approach is to close the loop between requirements an=
d their validators.
If a validator gives unstable (e.g., low-confidence or inconsistent) output=
s =E2=80=93 especially when compared to human annotations =E2=80=93 it like=
ly signals ambiguity or misalignment in the requirement itself.
To do so, future work can invest in meta evaluation and alignment of LLM-as=
-judge&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Shankar et&nbsp;al.=
 (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib33" title=3D"" clas=
s=3D"ltx_ref">2024</a>)</cite>, strategically have developers review (inten=
tionally curated) validation results, and develop strategies that can refin=
e the requirements.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"S6" class=3D"ltx_section">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id=3D"S6.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S6.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S6.p1.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_font_bold">Instruction following capabilities of LLMs.</span>
Much research has investigated LLMs=E2=80=99 instruction-following capabili=
ties, from building datasets&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cit=
e">Zhou et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib=
49" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2023</a>); Qin et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"htt=
ps://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib29" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">20=
24</a>)</cite>, training better models&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_cit=
emacro_cite">Sanh et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360=
v3#bib.bib31" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2021</a>); Ouyang et&nbsp;al. (<=
a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib28" title=3D"" class=
=3D"ltx_ref">2022</a>)</cite>, to optimizing for task-specific instruction-=
following capabilities&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Zha=
o et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib46" ti=
tle=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2024</a>)</cite>.
Instruction-following ensures LLMs meet specified requirements, but real-wo=
rld prompts are often underspecified =E2=80=93 studying underspecification =
makes LLMs not only usable (following instructions) but also reliable (robu=
st when underspecified).</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S6.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S6.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S6.p2.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_font_bold">Empirical analysis of LLM behaviors.</span>
Lots of work has empirically analyzed the behaviors of LLMs:
They found that LLMs are sensitive to prompt design&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx=
_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Sclar et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/h=
tml/2505.13360v3#bib.bib32" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2023</a>); Cao et&=
nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib9" title=3D=
"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2024</a>)</cite>, that different LLMs exhibit differen=
t behaviors&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Dunlap et&nbsp=
;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib13" title=3D"" =
class=3D"ltx_ref">2024</a>); Sun et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/=
html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib35" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2025</a>)</cite>,=
 and that LLM updates can often trigger unexpected performance regression&n=
bsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Chen et&nbsp;al. (<a href=
=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib10" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_=
ref">2024</a>); Ma et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.1336=
0v3#bib.bib24" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2024</a>)</cite>.
We also study LLMs=E2=80=99 robustness across prompt or model changes; howe=
ver, we specifically focus on their robustness on following unspecified req=
uirements, rather than specified tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S6.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S6.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S6.p3.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_font_bold">Ambiguity resolution when interacting with LLMs.</span>
Ambiguity detection and resolution are closely related to underspecificatio=
n.
For interactive applications, asking clarifying questions can be used as a =
fallback mechanism to resolve where user prompts are underspecified&nbsp;<c=
ite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Zhang and Choi (<a href=3D"https:=
//arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib44" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2023<=
/a>); Zhang et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib=
.bib45" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2024</a>)</cite>.
However, studies found that LLMs often struggle to detect ambiguity in user=
 queries&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Vijayvargiya et&n=
bsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib39" title=3D=
"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2025</a>); Ma et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.or=
g/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib23" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2025</a>); Laba=
n et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib20" ti=
tle=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2025</a>)</cite>, and much work has tried to imp=
rove LLMs=E2=80=99 abilities to handle ambiguity&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_ci=
te ltx_citemacro_cite">Kim et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2=
505.13360v3#bib.bib19" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"S6.p4" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S6.p4.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"S6.p4.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text =
ltx_font_bold">Prompt optimization.</span>
Prompt optimization has been extensively studied to adapt the prompt to imp=
rove LLM performance&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Khatt=
ab et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib18" t=
itle=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2023</a>); Yuksekgonul et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"=
https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib41" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref"=
>2024</a>); Li et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#=
bib.bib21" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2024</a>)</cite>.
They require a clear metric to guide the optimization.
Our work complements their work by focusing on <span id=3D"S6.p4.1.2" class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">identifying what metrics to optimize</span> t=
hrough properly discovering and operationalizing task requirements to const=
ruct a multi-faceted optimization objective.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"S7" class=3D"ltx_section">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id=3D"S7.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"S7.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">Prompt underspecification is common and c=
hallenging:
LLMs can infer missing requirements but behave inconsistently across prompt=
s and models, while fully specified requirements overwhelm them.
We show that requirements-aware optimizers make prompts easier to follow an=
d argue that systematic discovery, evaluation, and monitoring can help effe=
ctively manage underspecification.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"Sx1" class=3D"ltx_section">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id=3D"Sx1.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"Sx1.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"Sx1.p1.1.1" class=3D"ltx_tex=
t ltx_font_bold">Scale of experiments.</span>
Our experiments are conducted on a relatively small number of requirements =
(n=3D60) and synthetic prompts (n=3D240).
This setup allows us to curate high-quality, human-validated, realistic req=
uirement=E2=80=93validator pairs and study underspecification systematicall=
y, but it limits the breadth of our analysis.
To scale up, future work will need to curate a larger set of realistic requ=
irements and validators =E2=80=93 note that high-quality validators usually=
 require manual validation&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"=
>Shankar et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bi=
b33" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2024</a>)</cite>. They will also need mor=
e efficient ways (our experiments involved over 1.5 million LLM calls, see =
Appendix&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A1.SS9" title=
=3D"A.9 Compute resources used in the experiments =E2=80=A3 Appendix A Deta=
ils on Experiments Setups =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Underst=
anding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><s=
pan class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.9</span></a>) to produce fine-grained =
requirement-specific evaluation results while keeping the results reliable.=
</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"Sx1.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"Sx1.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"Sx1.p2.1.1" class=3D"ltx_tex=
t ltx_font_bold">Evaluation methodology.</span>
We rely on LLM validators to scale up the evaluation.
While we manually validate their reliability and ensure a small error rate,=
 LLM validators can exhibit preference biases toward models from the same f=
amily.
This, however, does not affect our main results, which do not compare acros=
s model families (e.g., <span id=3D"Sx1.p2.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_=
italic">=E2=80=9Cmodel updates regress more on unspecified requirements=E2=
=80=9D</span>).</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"Sx1.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"Sx1.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"Sx1.p3.1.1" class=3D"ltx_tex=
t ltx_font_bold">Connection to practices.</span>
Our primary goal in this work is to first establish an empirical understand=
ing of the underspecification problem.
Accordingly, we do not include a user study or direct integration with deve=
loper workflows.
This raises the possibility that the observed patterns may not transfer cle=
anly into practice.
Future work should evaluate how well these findings generalize when embedde=
d in real-world processes, constraints, and decision-making contexts of pro=
mpt development.</p>
</div>
</section>
