<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">=E2=80=A2</span>=20
<div id=3D"A1.I1.i2.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">product-gen</span>: Write engaging produc=
t descriptions from the provided product details.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li id=3D"A1.I1.i3" class=3D"ltx_item" style=3D"list-style-type:none;">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">=E2=80=A2</span>=20
<div id=3D"A1.I1.i3.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class=
=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">code-explain</span>: Explain a code snipp=
et for learning purposes.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"A1.SS2" class=3D"ltx_subsection">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Process of data sampl=
ing and cleaning</h3>

<div id=3D"A1.SS2.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS2.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">We re-purposed three existing dataset=
s to run the LLM+Prompts on: Commitpackft&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_=
citemacro_cite">Muennighoff et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/=
2505.13360v3#bib.bib26" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2023</a>)</cite> for c=
ode explanation (MIT license), a subset of UltraChat&nbsp;<cite class=3D"lt=
x_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Ding et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/h=
tml/2505.13360v3#bib.bib12" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2023</a>)</cite> f=
or trip advisory (MIT License), and Amazon ESCI&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cit=
e ltx_citemacro_cite">Reddy et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/=
2505.13360v3#bib.bib30" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2022</a>)</cite> for p=
roduct description generation (Apache-2.0 License).</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"A1.SS2.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<ul id=3D"A1.I2" class=3D"ltx_itemize">
<li id=3D"A1.I2.i1" class=3D"ltx_item" style=3D"list-style-type:none;">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">=E2=80=A2</span>=20
<div id=3D"A1.I2.i1.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">For Commitpackft, we take the pytho=
n split<span id=3D"footnote3" class=3D"ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup cla=
ss=3D"ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class=3D"ltx_note_outer"><span class=3D"l=
tx_note_content"><sup class=3D"ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class=3D"ltx_tag=
 ltx_tag_note">3</span><a href=3D"https://huggingface.co/datasets/bigcode/c=
ommitpackft" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https=
://huggingface.co/datasets/bigcode/commitpackft</a></span></span></span>, w=
e keep all examples with more than 90 lines of code (n=3D357), and then tak=
e the first 200 examples.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li id=3D"A1.I2.i2" class=3D"ltx_item" style=3D"list-style-type:none;">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">=E2=80=A2</span>=20
<div id=3D"A1.I2.i2.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">For UltraChat, we take a travel-rel=
ated subset<span id=3D"footnote4" class=3D"ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup=
 class=3D"ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class=3D"ltx_note_outer"><span class=
=3D"ltx_note_content"><sup class=3D"ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class=3D"lt=
x_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span><a href=3D"https://huggingface.co/datasets/soni=
awmeyer/travel-conversations-finetuning" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref ltx_ur=
l ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/datasets/soniawmeyer/travel-c=
onversations-finetuning</a></span></span></span> and keep the first 800 exa=
mples.
We then use <span id=3D"A1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewri=
ter">gpt-4o-mini</span> to label if each query is asking for travel recomme=
ndations, itineraries, and tips, and filter out the queries that are not (n=
=3D248 remains). We then take the first 200 examples.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li id=3D"A1.I2.i3" class=3D"ltx_item" style=3D"list-style-type:none;">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">=E2=80=A2</span>=20
<div id=3D"A1.I2.i3.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.I2.i3.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">For Amazon ESCI, we take the test s=
plit<span id=3D"footnote5" class=3D"ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class=
=3D"ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class=3D"ltx_note_outer"><span class=3D"ltx=
_note_content"><sup class=3D"ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class=3D"ltx_tag l=
tx_tag_note">5</span><a href=3D"https://huggingface.co/datasets/tasksource/=
esci" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://hugg=
ingface.co/datasets/tasksource/esci</a></span></span></span>, filter out th=
e examples that are not based in US and not in English, and remove duplicat=
ed products.
We then sample 200 examples from the dataset.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id=3D"A1.SS2.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS2.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p">From each dataset, we split it into t=
raining, validation, and test data with 15/35/50 split.
All our evaluation results are reported based on the test split.
All datasets are available in our shared code repository.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"A1.SS3" class=3D"ltx_subsection">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Process of requiremen=
ts curation</h3>

<div id=3D"A1.SS3.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS3.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">From each task, we curated an initial=
 list of requirements through the three different sources described in Sect=
ion&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S3" title=3D"3 How =
do LLM+Prompts Behave when Underspecified? =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=
=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" cla=
ss=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
We found existing prompts provided by Anthropic, Google and GPTs<span id=3D=
"footnote6" class=3D"ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class=3D"ltx_note_mar=
k">6</sup><span class=3D"ltx_note_outer"><span class=3D"ltx_note_content"><=
sup class=3D"ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</=
span><a href=3D"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/prompt-library/code-clarifier=
" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://docs.ant=
hropic.com/en/prompt-library/code-clarifier</a>,
<br class=3D"ltx_break"><a href=3D"https://github.com/google-marketing-solu=
tions/feedgen/blob/main/img/config.png" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref ltx_url=
 ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/google-marketing-solutions/feedgen=
/blob/main/img/config.png</a>,=20
<br class=3D"ltx_break"><a href=3D"https://github.com/yourzxb/GPTs/blob/mai=
n/17/TripAdvisor.md" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewrite=
r">https://github.com/yourzxb/GPTs/blob/main/17/TripAdvisor.md</a></span></=
span></span> (Figure&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A1=
.F7" title=3D"Figure 7 =E2=80=A3 A.3 Process of requirements curation =E2=
=80=A3 Appendix A Details on Experiments Setups =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=
=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompt=
s" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
We kept all requirements that are curated from existing prompt, as they alr=
eady approved by some human developers.</p>
</div>
<figure id=3D"A1.F5" class=3D"ltx_figure"><img src=3D"https://arxiv.org/htm=
l/2505.13360v3/x5.png" id=3D"A1.F5.g1" class=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centering =
ltx_img_landscape" width=3D"830" height=3D"353" alt=3D"Refer to caption">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>We gather 60 requirements for our analysis. Th=
e majority of requirements come from bottom-up error analysis (41.7%), foll=
owed by existing prompts (35%), and top-down brainstorming (23.3%).
Most requirements specify content-related constraints (75%), followed by st=
yle (16.7%) and format (8.3%).
Most requirements are global and apply to all examples (60%), while 40% are=
 conditional requirements.
We found that existing prompts rarely consider conditional requirements (on=
ly 14.3%).
</figcaption>
</figure>
