gure 6: </span>Sample annotator instruction during requirement curation.</f=
igcaption>
</figure>
<div id=3D"A1.SS3.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS3.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p">We use <span id=3D"A1.SS3.p2.1.1" cla=
ss=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o</span> to elicit requirements th=
rough brainstorming and error analysis.
The error analysis is conducted on the responses generated from a set of sm=
aller LLMs (<span id=3D"A1.SS3.p2.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewrite=
r">gpt-4o-mini</span>, <span id=3D"A1.SS3.p2.1.3" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_fon=
t_typewriter">gemini-1.5-flash</span>, <span id=3D"A1.SS3.p2.1.4" class=3D"=
ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">llama3.2-11b</span>).
For elicited requirements, we use <span id=3D"A1.SS3.p2.1.5" class=3D"ltx_t=
ext ltx_font_typewriter">text-embedding-ada-002</span> to generate embeddin=
gs of each requirement and remove ones with high cosine similarity (<math i=
d=3D"A1.SS3.p2.1.m1" class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"&gt;0.9" display=3D"inli=
ne" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi></mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.9</mn>=
</mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">&gt;0.9</annotation></sem=
antics></math>) to other existing requirements incrementally.
After this step, we curate 38, 39, and 40 requirements for <span id=3D"A1.S=
S3.p2.1.6" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">trip-advisory</span>, <sp=
an id=3D"A1.SS3.p2.1.7" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">product-gen<=
/span>, and <span id=3D"A1.SS3.p2.1.8" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewrite=
r">code-explain</span> tasks respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"A1.SS3.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS3.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p">We then filtered out the requirements=
 that are overly specific (e.g., <span id=3D"A1.SS3.p3.1.1" class=3D"ltx_te=
xt ltx_font_italic">=E2=80=9CThe output must explain how the product=E2=80=
=99s features enhance the karaoke experience for the targeted age group.=E2=
=80=9D</span>), and finally had three independent annotators select importa=
nt requirements.
We recruited the annotators from our contacts and ensured that they all hav=
e sufficient background knowledge for the task they need to annotate (e.g.,=
 all three annotators are in a computer science PhD program for the code ex=
planation task).
The annotators have access to a short annotation rubric, including an instr=
uction that asks them to mark up important requirements for the task and ad=
d justifications when needed, a task description, and example inputs to gro=
und their annotations (Figure&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.1=
3360v3#A1.F6" title=3D"Figure 6 =E2=80=A3 A.3 Process of requirements curat=
ion =E2=80=A3 Appendix A Details on Experiments Setups =E2=80=A3 What Promp=
ts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM =
Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a=
>).
The annotation process takes up to 20 minutes, and the annotators are not c=
ompensated for their time.
We verbally communicated to the annotators that their selected requirements=
 will be used for later experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"A1.SS3.p4" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS3.p4.1" class=3D"ltx_p">We kept the requirements selected by =
at least one human annotator in the end, with 20 requirements for each and =
a total of 60 requirements as in Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/=
html/2505.13360v3#A1.SS5" title=3D"A.5 Complete list of curated requirement=
s =E2=80=A3 Appendix A Details on Experiments Setups =E2=80=A3 What Prompts=
 Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Pr=
ompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a=
>.
Note that different annotators may diverge in the requirements they selecte=
d, and we intentionally kept the diversity from the process, as it is natur=
al that different stakeholders may have different priorities&nbsp;<cite cla=
ss=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Van&nbsp;Lamsweerde (<a href=3D"https://=
arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib38" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2009</a=
>)</cite>.
The final list of requirements covers different categories (content, style,=
 format), different scopes (global, conditional), as visualized in Figure&n=
bsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A1.F5" title=3D"Figure 5=
 =E2=80=A3 A.3 Process of requirements curation =E2=80=A3 Appendix A Detail=
s on Experiments Setups =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understan=
ding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><spa=
n class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"A1.SS3.p5" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS3.p5.1" class=3D"ltx_p">We provide all requirements used in o=
ur experiments in the shared code repository along with their validators.</=
p>
</div>
