<section id=3D"A1.SS6" class=3D"ltx_subsection">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.6 </span>Human validation of r=
equirement validators</h3>

<div id=3D"A1.SS6.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS6.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"A1.SS6.p1.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Validator construction.</span>
We employ a two-step procedure to construct reqiorement validators:
First, we have a planner (<span id=3D"A1.SS6.p1.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_=
font_typewriter">o3-mini</span>) to draft a step-by-step evaluation plan or=
 a Python script, for each requirement.
Next, a validator either executes the Python function, or the natural langu=
age evaluation plan (with <span id=3D"A1.SS6.p1.1.3" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_=
font_typewriter">gpt-4.1-mini</span>) on each example to produce the final =
judgment.
We iterate the validators for each requirement on the train split to achiev=
e high human-LLM agreement.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"A1.SS6.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS6.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p"><span id=3D"A1.SS6.p2.1.1" class=3D"l=
tx_text ltx_font_bold">Human validation.</span>
To assess the reliability of our LLM-based requirement evaluators, we curat=
ed a set of
1,095 evaluation results for human validation.
The evaluation results are curated with stratified sampling:
We sample 20 evaluation results (10 positive, 10 negative) per requirement.
If there are not enough results (e.g., when a requirement is almost always =
satisfied), we take the maximum number available.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"A1.SS6.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS6.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p">We then have a human annotator manual=
ly review the evaluations.
For each model output, the annotator compared the predicted label against t=
heir own judgment of whether the output satisfied the given requirement.
Overall, the evaluators have 95.6% (SD=3D0.08) agreement rates, indicating =
a reasonably high level of human=E2=80=93LLM agreement.</p>
</div>
</section>
