<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 11: </span>We use a cyclic design to generate prompts. E=
ach prompt (row) covers the same number of <math id=3D"A1.F11.4.m1" class=
=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"k" display=3D"inline" intent=3D":literal"><semanti=
cs><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">k</annotation></sem=
antics></math> consecutive requirements (column). Each requirement is speci=
fied <math id=3D"A1.F11.5.m2" class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"k" display=3D"i=
nline" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=3D"app=
lication/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times and unspecified <ma=
th id=3D"A1.F11.6.m3" class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"N-k" display=3D"inline"=
 intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=E2=88=92</mo><mi>k</mi=
></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">N-k</annotation></semant=
ics></math> times exactly. We randomize the order of requirements to distri=
bute requirements from different sources.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id=3D"A1.SS7.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS7.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p">In our experiments, our goal is to sy=
stematically cover requirement subsets, to make sure (a) each prompt has ro=
ughly the same complexity in terms of the number of requirements to follow,=
 and (b) different requirements are specified or unspecified the same numbe=
r of times.
We use a simple cyclic design to achieve this, as shown in Figure&nbsp;<a h=
ref=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A1.F11" title=3D"Figure 11 =E2=
=80=A3 A.7 Prompt templates and construction =E2=80=A3 Appendix A Details o=
n Experiments Setups =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understandin=
g and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span c=
lass=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"A1.SS8" class=3D"ltx_subsection">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.8 </span>LLM judge setups for =
generic prompt optimizers</h3>

<div id=3D"A1.SS8.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS8.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">For generic prompt optimizers, we use=
d a requirement-agnostic LLM judge.
The judge is provided with task-level information, prompt instruction, as w=
ell as inputs and outputs from the task.
The judge then rates the output on a 1-to-10 scale.
We use <span id=3D"A1.SS8.p1.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gp=
t-4.1-mini</span> as the judge with a temperature of 0 to ensure judge cons=
istency, and verified that this model provides sufficiently consistent and =
reasonable judgments for our tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"A1.SS9" class=3D"ltx_subsection">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.9 </span>Compute resources use=
d in the experiments</h3>

<div id=3D"A1.SS9.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS9.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">In our first set of experiments (up t=
o Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S3.SS3" title=
=3D"3.3 LLMs are more likely to regress on unspecified requirements when up=
dated =E2=80=A3 3 How do LLM+Prompts Behave when Underspecified? =E2=80=A3 =
What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecificati=
on in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3=
.3</span></a>), we make 6k inferences with each of the 7 models to obtain m=
odel outputs. We then make 840k inferences to evaluate the results with <sp=
an id=3D"A1.SS9.p1.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4.1-mini=
</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"A1.SS9.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS9.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p">In our second experiment (Section&nbs=
p;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S3.SS4" title=3D"3.4 LLMs =
struggle with following many requirements at the same time =E2=80=A3 3 How =
do LLM+Prompts Behave when Underspecified? =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=
=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" cla=
ss=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>), we mak=
e 6k inferences with each of the two models on 3 different prompt configura=
tions (different numbers of requirements). We then make 720k inferences to =
evaluate the results with <span id=3D"A1.SS9.p2.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_=
font_typewriter">gpt-4.1-mini</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"A1.SS9.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS9.p3.1" class=3D"ltx_p">For prompt optimization experiments (=
Section&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#S4" title=3D"4 =
Requirements-Aware Prompt Optimization =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t=
 Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=
=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), we make 16=
.2k inferences with each prompt optimizer to produce all optimized prompts =
(324k inferences for evaluation).
We then make 6k inferences with 4 different optimization results each (480k=
 inferences for evaluation).</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"A1.SS9.p4" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A1.SS9.p4.1" class=3D"ltx_p">For LLM validators, we used a tempera=
ture of 0 to extract the most likely prediction. For other inference calls,=
 we used the recommended temperature in best practices (1.0 for <span id=3D=
"A1.SS9.p4.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o</span> models=
 and 0.6 for <span id=3D"A1.SS9.p4.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_typewrit=
er">Llama-3</span> models.) The maximum tokens are set to 4096, and all oth=
er inference parameters are set to default values.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id=3D"A2" class=3D"ltx_appendix">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Detailed Analysi=
s on Requirements Discovery</h2>

<figure id=3D"A2.F12" class=3D"ltx_figure"><img src=3D"https://arxiv.org/ht=
ml/2505.13360v3/x7.png" id=3D"A2.F12.g1" class=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centerin=
g ltx_img_landscape" width=3D"664" height=3D"339" alt=3D"Refer to caption">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 12: </span>To discover an unspecified requirement reliab=
ly with 95% probability, developers need to inspect a lot more examples (<m=
ath id=3D"A2.F12.3.m1" class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"N\uparrow" display=3D"=
inline" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=3D"fals=
e">=E2=86=91</mo><mi></mi></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex"=
>N\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>) when the requirement appears le=
ss frequently, gets violated less, or is harder to detect (<math id=3D"A2.F=
12.4.m2" class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"p\downarrow" display=3D"inline" inte=
nt=3D":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">=E2=86=93=
</mo><mi></mi></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">p\downarrow=
</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id=3D"A2.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A2.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">If underspecified prompts are more unstab=
le, could developers discover <span id=3D"A2.p1.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_=
font_italic">relevant</span> task requirements in the first place?
We argue that this is rather challenging with current manual trial-and-erro=
r prompt engineering practices,
where developers examine examples in an ad-hoc fashion and iterate their pr=
ompts only when they observe outputs that violate their expectations&nbsp;<=
cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">Liang et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"ht=
tps://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#bib.bib22" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2=
024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"A2.p2" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A2.p2.1" class=3D"ltx_p">First, many requirements are <span id=3D"=
A2.p2.1.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">conditional</span> and can ea=
sily be missed when developers only look at a few representative examples =
=E2=80=93 for example, <span id=3D"A2.p2.1.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_it=
alic">=E2=80=9Caccurate numerical values in summaries=E2=80=9D</span> is on=
ly relevant to inputs containing numerical values.
When the probability of encountering such inputs (<math id=3D"A2.p2.1.m1" c=
lass=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"p_{\text{relevant}}" display=3D"inline" intent=
=3D":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>relevant</mtext></msub><ann=
otation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">p_{\text{relevant}}</annotation></se=
mantics></math>) is low, the requirement is likely not to be covered within=
 a few inspections.
However, our previous analysis demonstrates that conditional requirements a=
re exactly where LLMs struggle more.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"A2.p3" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A2.p3.2" class=3D"ltx_p">Second, some requirements may be violated=
 less frequently (<math id=3D"A2.p3.1.m1" class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"p_{=
\text{violated}}" display=3D"inline" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><msub><=
mi>p</mi><mtext>violated</mtext></msub><annotation encoding=3D"application/=
x-tex">p_{\text{violated}}</annotation></semantics></math>), and thus less =
likely to be discovered via observing violations.
Yet they can still be critical, such as high-stakes safety requirements for=
 trip advisory =E2=80=93 e.g., <span id=3D"A2.p3.2.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx=
_font_italic">=E2=80=9Cno dangerous activities suggested.=E2=80=9D</span>
In other cases, violations may be harder to recognize, with a lower probabi=
lity of being noticed (<math id=3D"A2.p3.2.m2" class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=
=3D"p_{\text{noticed}}" display=3D"inline" intent=3D":literal"><semantics><=
msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>noticed</mtext></msub><annotation encoding=3D"applica=
tion/x-tex">p_{\text{noticed}}</annotation></semantics></math>), as in <spa=
n id=3D"A2.p3.2.2" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">=E2=80=9Censuring cor=
rect program execution in code explanations.=E2=80=9D</span>
Moreover, when developers inspect LLM outputs, their assessments are biased=
 by prior knowledge and subjective interpretation, which can lead them to o=
verlook certain types of requirements&nbsp;<cite class=3D"ltx_cite ltx_cite=
macro_cite">Szymanski et&nbsp;al. (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.1=
3360v3#bib.bib36" title=3D"" class=3D"ltx_ref">2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"A2.p4" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A2.p4.2" class=3D"ltx_p">Considering these factors, an unspecified=
 requirement may require significant efforts or luck to be discovered with =
the current practice.
Quantitatively,
a developer will need to look at <math id=3D"A2.p4.1.m1" class=3D"ltx_Math"=
 alttext=3D"N=3D\log(1-p_{s})/\log(1-p_{\text{relevant}}\cdot p_{\text{viol=
ated}}\cdot p_{\text{noticed}})" display=3D"inline" intent=3D":literal"><se=
mantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=3D</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>=E2=81=A1</=
mo><mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=E2=88=92</mo><ms=
ub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow></=
mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>=E2=81=A1</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=3D"fa=
lse">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=E2=88=92</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>r=
elevant</mtext></msub><mo lspace=3D"0.222em" rspace=3D"0.222em">=E2=8B=85</=
mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>violated</mtext></msub><mo lspace=3D"0.222em" rsp=
ace=3D"0.222em">=E2=8B=85</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>noticed</mtext></msub>=
</mrow></mrow><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><ann=
otation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">N=3D\log(1-p_{s})/\log(1-p_{\text{re=
levant}}\cdot p_{\text{violated}}\cdot p_{\text{noticed}})</annotation></se=
mantics></math> examples to discover the requirement with probability <math=
 id=3D"A2.p4.2.m2" class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"p_{s}" display=3D"inline" =
intent=3D":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation=
 encoding=3D"application/x-tex">p_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g.=
, 0.95), assuming independent Bernoulli trials.
For example, a <span id=3D"A2.p4.2.1" class=3D"ltx_text ltx_font_italic">co=
nditional</span> requirement that is relevant to 20% of examples, violated =
50% of the time, and perfectly noticeable will require inspecting more than=
 29 examples to be detected with 95% probability (Figure&nbsp;<a href=3D"ht=
tps://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A2.F12" title=3D"Figure 12 =E2=80=A3 Appe=
ndix B Detailed Analysis on Requirements Discovery =E2=80=A3 What Prompts D=
on=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prom=
pts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).
This will be an excessive workload for a human to complete on their own for=
 every single model update or prompt change,
assuming they have access to a diverse dataset, if at all.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id=3D"A3" class=3D"ltx_appendix">
<h2 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Additional exper=
iment results</h2>

<figure id=3D"A3.F13" class=3D"ltx_figure"><img src=3D"https://arxiv.org/ht=
ml/2505.13360v3/x8.png" id=3D"A3.F13.g1" class=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centerin=
g ltx_img_landscape" width=3D"830" height=3D"197" alt=3D"Refer to caption">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>Comparing LLM+Prompts performances on specifi=
ed requirements vs. unspecified requirements, we found that, overall, LLM+P=
rompts perform worse and diverge more for unspecified requirements. This is=
 statistically significant even if we consider all other factors and explai=
ns a large portion of the variances observed (Table&nbsp;<a href=3D"https:/=
/arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A3.T4" title=3D"Table 4 =E2=80=A3 C.2 ANOVA re=
sults =E2=80=A3 Appendix C Additional experiment results =E2=80=A3 What Pro=
mpts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Understanding and Managing Underspecification in LL=
M Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span><=
/a>).</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id=3D"A3.F14" class=3D"ltx_figure"><img src=3D"https://arxiv.org/ht=
ml/2505.13360v3/x9.png" id=3D"A3.F14.g1" class=3D"ltx_graphics ltx_centerin=
g ltx_img_landscape" width=3D"830" height=3D"341" alt=3D"Refer to caption">
<figcaption class=3D"ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_=
tag_figure">Figure 14: </span>The histogram of average requirement accuracy=
 drops when the prompts include more requirements (N=3D1<math id=3D"A3.F14.=
2.m1" class=3D"ltx_Math" alttext=3D"\rightarrow" display=3D"inline" intent=
=3D":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy=3D"false">=E2=86=92</mo><annotation e=
ncoding=3D"application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>19=
). We found 37.5% requirements drop significantly by more than 5%.</figcapt=
ion>
</figure>
<section id=3D"A3.SS1" class=3D"ltx_subsection">
<h3 class=3D"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">
<span class=3D"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Requirement guess rat=
es breakdown</h3>

<div id=3D"A3.SS1.p1" class=3D"ltx_para">
<p id=3D"A3.SS1.p1.1" class=3D"ltx_p">We break down how often LLMs can =E2=
=80=9Cguess=E2=80=9D requirements and satisfy them without specification (T=
able&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13360v3#A3.T3" title=3D"Ta=
ble 3 =E2=80=A3 C.1 Requirement guess rates breakdown =E2=80=A3 Appendix C =
Additional experiment results =E2=80=A3 What Prompts Don=E2=80=99t Say: Und=
erstanding and Managing Underspecification in LLM Prompts" class=3D"ltx_ref=
"><span class=3D"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
We found that LLMs excel at global, format-related, and prompt-sourced requ=
irements, but struggle with conditional, style-related, and bottom-up requi=
rements.</p>
</div>
<figure id=3D"A3.T3" class=3D"ltx_table">
<table id=3D"A3.T3.1" class=3D"ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_header=
s ltx_align_middle">
